    "q-221923": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221923",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\nI. $3∣(14x+21y)$\n\nII. $7∣(14x+21y)$\n\nIII. $4∣(14x+21y)$",
      "worked_solution": "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $14(1)+21(0)=14,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n14x+21y=7(2x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $7∣(14x+21y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $14(1)+21(0)=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
