    "q-221930": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221930",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\nI. $6$ divides $(15x+30y)$\n\nII. $5$ divides $(15x+30y)$\n\nIII. $15$ divides $(15x+30y)$",
      "worked_solution": "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ we have $15(-1)+30(1)=15,$ which is not divisible by $6.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n15x+30y=5(3x+6y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $3x+6y$ must be an integer, too. So, $5$ divides $(15x+30y).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n15x+30y=15(x+2y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $x+2y$ must be an integer, too. So, $15$ divides $(15x+30y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
